Z||000000||learner||08-30-99||09:56 PM||||Has anyone successfully replaced original struts with adjustable system on the rear of 86 GT?
Z||000001||mwbackus||08-30-99||11:00 PM||||I intend on replacing my struts (formula) in the next few days.  I was recently shopping around for some prices on KYB's, and I found something that was very peculiar on the summit racing web site.  The search for the GR-2's came up with the following:<p>"The part KYB-234005 is considered hazardous and can not be ordered online at this time."<p>Perhaps I should reconsider my little project..  :-)<p>
Z||000002||lowCG||08-31-99||12:23 AM||||If you can get ahold of some VW Rabbit/Golf strut housings,the tops can be cut off and welded onto(carefully)the stock Fiero housings,to hold any cartridge,my choice being the Bilstien Sport units for('85-'92)Golf's,they're currently $96 each from Nuespeed.<br>Actually,there's a groove machined inside of the Fiero strut,about an inch and a Quarter from the very top,that makes for a good reference point for construction.The Bilstein should stick up out of the housing .10 of an inch,before the collar/retainer is put on.
Z||000003||VISCERAL||08-31-99||08:13 AM||||Yeah, I got koni adjustables on the back with a coilover kit w/ an eibach spring from groundcontrol.com. I was able drop the back about 1.5" I chose a 350# spring. Too harsh.<br>Maybe a 275 or 300# spring would be better. Try getting the kyb or bilstein shocks. Get the adjustable front shocks and loweing springs and rear sway bar. It's great. BTW I know a site that can fabricate Dropped spindles for 84-87 fieros<p>http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/
